WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.400
So, where was W'Kabi? The character's absence from&nbsp;
"Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" had more to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.240
with the busy schedule of the actor playing&nbsp;
him than the character himself, it seems.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.560
In the months leading up to the November 2022&nbsp;
release of "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.200
Marvel Cinematic Universe fans learned relatively&nbsp;
little about the film's plot or cast. It also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.400
wasn't totally clear how "Wakanda Forever"&nbsp;
would ultimately handle the tragic 2020 death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.500
of Chadwick Boseman, who played King T'Challa,&nbsp;
the title character from 2018's "Black Panther."

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.660
But fans were comforted by the information&nbsp;
they did get. Marvel confirmed that many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.980
the stars of "Black Panther" would return in&nbsp;
"Wakanda Forever," including Letitia Wright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.700
who plays Shuri; Lupita Nyong'o,&nbsp;
who plays Nakia; and Martin Freeman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.380
who portrays CIA agent Everett Ross. The movie&nbsp;
also introduces a number of new characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:52.320
such as Tenoch Huerta's Namor, Dominique Thorne's&nbsp;
Riri Williams, and Michaela Cole's Aneka.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.480
But not everyone from the first movie returns&nbsp;
in the sequel. Indeed, one of the most memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.220
supporting characters from the first "Black&nbsp;
Panther" film is discussed, but isn't present.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.020
In a Tweet that is no longer public, Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes Awards Editor Jacqueline Coley first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.700
revealed that actor Daniel Kaluuya did not&nbsp;
film any scenes as W'Kabi for "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.360
Wakanda Forever." Coley said that Kaluuya&nbsp;
mentioned in an interview that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.960
unable to reprise his "Black Panther"&nbsp;
role because of a scheduling conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.220
with director Jordan Peele's film "Nope."&nbsp;
Variety later confirmed this to be true.

00:01:23.387 --> 00:01:26.987
"It's a shame. But it's, um--&nbsp;
I'm so happy they're doing it."

00:01:26.987 --> 00:01:32.040
Kaluuya plays one of the lead characters in&nbsp;
"Nope," and the film is his first collaboration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.740
with Peele since 2017's "Get Out." It seems&nbsp;
the monumental re-teaming of actor and director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.240
meant Kaluuya had to pass up the opportunity&nbsp;
to appear in "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever."

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.900
Nevertheless, "Wakanda Forever"&nbsp;
weathers Kaluuya's absence well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.580
tying it into the events of the original&nbsp;
2018 film. In that movie, after being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.380
initially introduced as a major supporter of King&nbsp;
T'Challa, Kaluuya's W'Kabi turns on the king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.268
as well as his lover Okoye, to support Erik&nbsp;
"Killmonger" Stevens' take over of Wakanda.

00:02:00.268 --> 00:02:01.620
"Everything is on schedule."

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.742
"Have the spies been alerted?"

00:02:02.742 --> 00:02:05.319
"Yes. Some resistance to our new mission."

00:02:05.319 --> 00:02:09.780
Of course, Killmonger's coup ultimately&nbsp;
fails, and Okoye eventually confronts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.800
W'Kabi on the battlefield. W'Kabi has&nbsp;
no choice but to surrender to her.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.590
"Would you kill me, my love?"

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:16.260
"For Wakanda?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.779
Without question."

00:02:21.779 --> 00:02:27.000
So even if Kaluuya had been able to appear in&nbsp;
"Wakanda Forever," it's clear W'Kabi's actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.740
in the first movie would likely have resulted&nbsp;
in him having a reduced role in the sequel.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.480
W'Kabi is absent from "Wakanda Forever,"&nbsp;
but he does come up in dialogue once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.740
in the scene in which Queen Ramonda strips&nbsp;
Okoye of her position as general of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:45.000
Dora Milaje. Ramonda essentially implies&nbsp;
that he's been imprisoned for treason.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.200
Speaking to CinemaBlend, "Black Panther"&nbsp;
and "Wakanda Forever" director Ryan Coogler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.700
revealed just what W'Kabi is up to during&nbsp;
the events of "Wakanda Forever," saying,

00:03:01.943 --> 00:03:06.060
From the sound of it, W'Kabi is currently&nbsp;
something of an afterthought as far as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.420
ongoing story of the "Black Panther"&nbsp;
universe is concerned. But Coogler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.640
has confirmed that W'Kabi is both alive and&nbsp;
in Wakanda. That leaves room for his return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.680
in either a future "Black Panther" film or&nbsp;
Coogler's upcoming Wakanda-based Disney+ show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.700
should Kaluuya's schedule once again&nbsp;
allow him to reprise his MCU character.

